     2	"""ceo-cost-transcripts.py — cost/token report derived from harness-native
     3	transcripts (``message.usage`` per assistant turn), NOT the audit log.
     4	
     5	## Why this exists (PLAN-186 W0, S339)
     6	
     7	``ceo-cost.py`` and ``budget-summary.py``'s audit-log rollup report
     8	``$0.00`` over 30 days because the audit log is a *governance* log (spawns,
     9	vetoes, edits, ceremonies) — it is not a token ledger. The real spend lives
    10	in the harness-native transcripts under
    11	``~/.claude/projects/<slug>/*.jsonl`` (the main/"assento" sessions) and
    12	``~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session>/subagents/**/agent-*.jsonl`` (spawned
    13	sub-agents, both plain Task-tool and Workflow rails). Each ``type ==
    14	"assistant"`` record carries a full ``message.usage`` snapshot: fresh
    15	input, output, and prompt-cache read/write (split 5-minute / 1-hour TTL).
    16	
    17	This is documented in ``docs/research/s339-orchestrator-study/
    18	05-finops-routing.md`` (P0-1, §1, §Metodologia) — that report's own
    19	scratchpad-local aggregator produced **$11,137.97** over 2026-08-03 →
    20	2026-09-02. This script is the in-tree, tested, stdlib-only version of
    21	that instrument.
    22	
    23	``budget-summary.py`` already reads *some* native transcripts
    24	(``_read_native_spawn`` / ``collect_native_spawns``, lines 920-1200) but
    25	only under ``<session>/subagents/**`` — it never reads the top-level
    26	``<session>.jsonl`` files, so it is structurally blind to the "assento"
    27	(main session) spend, which the S339 report measured at 71% of the total.
    28	This script reads BOTH trees and separates them.
    29	
    30	## Pricing contract
    31	
    32	Default pricing is an EMBEDDED table (``_EMBEDDED_PRICING`` below),
    33	sourced from the S339 report §1.2/§1.4, itself derived from
    34	``docs/provider-pricing.md`` (primary table + cache-tier multipliers,
    35	lines ~130-153) and ``budget-summary.py``'s
    36	``_CACHE_READ_MULTIPLIER_OVERRIDES`` (the per-model cache-read
    37	exceptions, mirrored below; every other model at 0.10x base; cache
    38	WRITE is 1.25x base at the 5-minute TTL and 2.00x base at the 1-hour
    39	TTL — these multipliers are a
    40	structural constant, not something ``cost-table.yaml`` carries).
    41	
    42	``--pricing PATH`` (default ``cost-table.yaml`` next to this script)
    43	attempts to load base ``input_per_mtok`` / ``output_per_mtok`` rows from
    44	that file's mini-YAML ``models:`` block. Two cases:
    45	
    46	- **Format doesn't match at all** (file missing, unreadable, no
    47	  ``models:`` section found, zero rows extracted): fall back to the
    48	  fully embedded table wholesale. ``cost-table.yaml`` in this repo has
    49	  NO cache-read/cache-write columns at all — this instrument's whole
    50	  point is cache-aware pricing, so a table that cannot express that is,
    51	  by construction, a format mismatch for this use case; the embedded
    52	  table is the one that can.
    53	- **Format parses AND the path is the untouched default**: rows load
    54	  from the file, then the Owner-ratified corrections in
    55	  ``_RATIFIED_OVERRIDES_FOR_DEFAULT_TABLE`` are layered on top — but
    56	  ONLY where the parsed row actually DIFFERS from the ratified rate.
    57	  As of ``b6dce78`` the in-tree ``cost-table.yaml`` already carries the
    58	  ratified ``claude-sonnet-5`` $2/$10 row, so on the default path the
    59	  correction is currently a NO-OP and ``source`` says so. An
    60	  unconditional overwrite would be worse than useless: it would print
    61	  "ratified correction" while correcting nothing, and the day the
    62	  table is legitimately refreshed it would silently mask the new rate.
    63	  An EXPLICIT ``--pricing`` path supplied by the caller is trusted
    64	  as-is, with no correction at all — the caller opted into a specific
    65	  pricing config on purpose.
    66	
    67	Cache-read/write multipliers are ALWAYS the structural constants above,
    68	regardless of which base table is in play, applied per-model (the
    69	``_CACHE_READ_MULTIPLIER_OVERRIDES`` exceptions act on cache read only).
    70	
    71	## Corpus contract
    72	
    73	- Dedup key: ``message.id`` when present (an API response's usage
    74	  snapshot is identical across every content-block JSONL line the
    75	  harness writes for that one message — thinking/text/tool_use blocks
    76	  each get their own line, all carrying the SAME ``message.usage``);
    77	  falls back to ``requestId`` + record ``uuid`` when ``message.id`` is
    78	  absent. The S339 report's methodology names a
    79	  ``(requestId, apiBlockIndex, message.id)`` key; ``apiBlockIndex`` was
    80	  not observed in this harness version's on-disk schema, so the key
    81	  degrades to what IS observed without inventing a field.
    82	- Role: "assento" for non-sidechain assistant turns in a top-level
    83	  ``<session>.jsonl``; "subagent" for every assistant turn under
    84	  ``<session>/subagents/**/agent-*.jsonl`` regardless of its own
    85	  ``isSidechain`` value (path-shape is the classifier, mirroring
    86	  ``budget-summary.py``'s rail-by-path doctrine — the corpus has no
    87	  reliable field for this). A sidechain turn inside a top-level file (not
    88	  observed in this corpus, but not structurally impossible) is skipped
    89	  rather than double-counted: any Task-tool sub-dispatch already has its
    90	  own dedicated ``agent-*.jsonl``.
    91	- Never touches ``iterations`` on a usage record (it double-counts
    92	  retries — same exclusion the S339 report's methodology names).
    93	- A JSON-parse failure on a candidate line is skipped and counted, never
    94	  raised. An ``unresolved`` model id (not found in the active pricing
    95	  table, after stripping a trailing ``[..]`` context-window suffix such
    96	  as ``[1m]``) is bucketed and reported with its token totals — never
    97	  silently priced at $0 as if it were a genuine zero-cost turn, and never
    98	  guessed.
    99	
   100	## Routing invariant (PLAN-186 AC-13, S361)
   101	
   102	EVERY execution of this instrument also ASSERTS, from runtime data only,
   103	that the model a spawn was SERVED equals the model its call site DECLARED
   104	(``transcript_rollup()['routing']``: the CLI report, the ``--json`` payload,
   105	and ``collect()``/``render_block()`` for ``ceo-cost.py`` and
   106	``budget-summary.py``). ADR-144:140 measured ``agent(..., {model})`` routing
   107	on ONE harness build (n=2) and carries no forward guarantee; a proof taken
   108	once at land time cannot see the harness going back to ``inherit`` later.
   109	
   110	- DECLARED is the ``model`` key of the spawn's ``agent-<id>.meta.json``
   111	  sidecar: what the call actually passed. It is never read from the text of
   112	  a script (an instrument that predicts code from text does not converge).
   113	  Absent or ``inherit`` means NO claim at the site: counted, never a
   114	  violation.
   115	- SERVED is ``message.model`` of the paired ``agent-<id>.jsonl``, never the
   116	  agent's self-report, read through the SAME ``scan_files()``/``dedup()`` as
   117	  the cost report -- with ONE difference: the served model is a fact of the
   118	  TURN, not of its bill, so the routing read lifts the ``usage``
   119	  requirement. A turn the cost report drops for want of ``usage`` (null,
   120	  absent, empty) is still read here, and an assistant record that cannot be
   121	  read at all is counted (INCONCLUSIVE), never silently skipped. Chunks of
   122	  one message resolve to the TERMINAL chunk (highest ``output_tokens``; on a
   123	  tie the LATER line in file order). A message whose chunks disagree about
   124	  the model is counted in ``chunk_model_splits`` (informational) when the
   125	  terminal chunk can be elected from complete information; if one of those
   126	  chunks has NO ``output_tokens`` the terminal cannot be proven, it is
   127	  counted in ``chunk_terminal_unprovable``, the group is EXCLUDED from the
   128	  comparison (its guessed terminal can neither raise a violation nor vouch
   129	  for the routing) and the verdict is INCONCLUSIVE -- never GREEN, and never
   130	  RED on that group's account; a RED can only come from another group whose
   131	  terminal is proven. ``<synthetic>`` is a harness pseudo-model and is
   132	  ignored.
   133	- EVERY string taken from a transcript or a sidecar is made printable where
   134	  it enters the instrument (non-printable characters become ``?``): a model
   135	  id, a session id, a label or an archetype name cannot forge a verdict line
   136	  or drive a terminal in ANY renderer.
   137	- A WINDOW decides first. A sidecar the check cannot trust (unreadable,
   138	  refused, or a declaration it cannot classify) gates a window only if its
   139	  spawn has a turn inside it; one from outside is counted as
   140	  ``out_of_window`` and never reaches the verdict. The same holds record by
   141	  record: an unreadable assistant record with a valid timestamp OUTSIDE the
   142	  window leaves before it is counted; only what has no temporal position at
   143	  all (a torn line, no timestamp) or sits inside the window can make the
   144	  verdict INCONCLUSIVE, and where that makes a spawn's placement unprovable
   145	  the check fails closed. The ``declared`` / ``undeclared`` counters stay
   146	  corpus-wide.
   147	- LABEL is the sidecar ``description`` (the Workflow ``label``; the native
   148	  spawn's ``description``).
   149	- ALIAS -> FAMILY, not alias -> id. ``opus|sonnet|haiku|fable`` are matched
   150	  by FAMILY, derived from the fleet tables and never retyped; a full id is
   151	  matched EXACTLY (a ``-YYYYMMDD`` date suffix is tolerated). A pinned
   152	  alias -> id table was refused on evidence: on one harness the same
   153	  ``sonnet`` was served as ``claude-sonnet-5`` and later as
   154	  ``claude-sonnet-5-5``, so such a table is red the day the substrate
   155	  re-points the alias and green over the very hole it has to see. The
   156	  alias -> served pairs actually observed are PRINTED, never judged.
   157	- SECOND PREDICATE (the AC-10 requirement named for whoever closes AC-13):
   158	  a spawn whose archetype (``customAgentType``, else ``agentType``) is in
   159	  ``VETO_FLOOR_ROLES`` must be served inside ``VETO_FLOOR_ALLOWED`` --
   160	  MEMBERSHIP, never a family prefix -- declared or not. Both sets are
   161	  imported from ``_lib.agent_frontmatter``, never retyped. Declared ==
   162	  served is silent about the floor (``model: sonnet`` on a VETO spawn
   163	  matches itself and is still a breach), which is why this is a second
   164	  predicate and not a corollary of the first.
   165	- Verdicts, worst first: RED (a violation), INCONCLUSIVE (something the
   166	  check could not read or classify, named), VACUOUS (nothing was compared:
   167	  a vacuous GREEN is refused), GREEN. The exit code stays 0 so the cost
   168	  report is never broken by a routing finding; ``--assert-routing`` turns
   169	  the verdict into a gate: 0 GREEN, 1 RED, 3 not proven (INCONCLUSIVE or
   170	  VACUOUS). Out of scope, declared: the seat (no sidecar), spawns that
   171	  declare nothing, an archetype that the sidecar does not record (the
   172	  persona-injected ``general-purpose`` rail), and whether a site's
   173	  ``model:`` is the RIGHT one for its task class (AC-3a / W1).
   174	
   175	PRE-REGISTERED DEATH CRITERIA -- fixed here before the first real run;
   176	changing any number is a plan amendment, never an edit in passing.
   177	
   178	  K-A (the detector dies: rebuild or retire it, never tune around it)
   179	    K-A1 positive control: the synthetic pair in
   180	         ``test_ceo_cost_transcripts.py`` (declared ``sonnet`` and served
   181	         an Opus id is RED; declared == served is GREEN) must hold on every
   182	         CI run. A control that stops biting kills the detector.
   183	    K-A2 blindness: an execution over a window with >= 20 spawn sidecars
   184	         and ``declared == 0``, in a window the operator knows contained
   185	         spawns that passed ``model:``, means the sidecar schema drifted
   186	         under the detector. It is blind until rebuilt against the new
   187	         schema. (The ``sidecars`` / ``declared`` / ``undeclared`` counters
   188	         are printed on every run so this is checkable from the output.)
   189	    K-A3 noise: three DISTINCT violations (three different sidecars) each
   190	         judged a FALSE POSITIVE of the predicate IN WRITING means the
   191	         predicate that fired is re-scoped by an ADR or deleted. A TRUE
   192	         positive is not noise: a deliberate below-floor probe, or a
   193	         harness fallback that really served another model, stays RED for
   194	         as long as its spawn is inside the window. There is deliberately
   195	         no suppression list in this file.
   196	  K-B (what a RED means: the lever, not the detector)
   197	    K-B1 a MISMATCH (no served turn on the declared model) on a site that
   198	         declares ``model:``, reproduced by ONE control spawn with the same
   199	         declaration on the same harness build, means ``model:`` does not
   200	         route on that build: the explicit sites of W1/AC-3a are inert
   201	         there, ADR-144's measurement is amended, and the premise "route
   202	         builders by ``model:``" is suspended until a build whose control
   203	         passes.
   204	    K-B2 one FLOOR_BREACH restarts, from zero, the PLAN-186 W3
   205	         end-of-transition clock ("one full wave with no floor violation
   206	         registered by the permanent detector").
   207	
