   199	
   200	## §5. Forensic vs blocking decoupling
   201	
   202	The framework ships **two** Bash canonical-write detectors with
   203	intentionally different lifecycles + responsibilities:
   204	
   205	| Aspect | `check_bash_safety.py` (PreToolUse) | `check_bash_canonical_forensic.py` (PostToolUse) |
   206	|---|---|---|
   207	| Phase | PreToolUse Bash | PostToolUse Bash |
   208	| Blocks? | **YES** (fail-CLOSED) | **NO** (always allow) |
   209	| Detection set | matrix v2 (31/34 rows; not the §6.6 class) | regex v1 (`>`, `>>`, `tee`, `sed -i`), literal canonical target only |
   210	| Audit event | none on a canonical-path block; `veto_triggered(bash_parse_failed_fail_closed)` on parse failure | `canonical_edit_completed` breadcrumb |
   211	| False-positive cost | command rejected — user retries | log line emitted — no user impact |
   212	| False-negative cost | bypass succeeds — 16 of 19 v1 gaps closed (15 by Wave B.3, row 33 at S207) | log line missed — forensic gap |
   213	| Decision latency target | ≤50ms p95 | <1ms p99 (regex-only, no dep import) |
   214	
   215	**Why two?** Belt-and-suspenders:
   216	
   217	- **The blocker has gaps** (§6). The forensic detector is NOT a
   218	  backstop for them. It matches only four literal write shapes in the
   219	  same raw command text, so it never sees the §6 rows, Class A or a
   220	  computed target. It classifies a target with the Edit/Write classifier
   221	  (`check_bash_canonical_forensic.py:63-69` → `check_canonical_edit._is_canonical`),
   222	  which resolves the path and folds ASCII case. By code reading, it can
   223	  log a respelled or symlinked literal target after the fact, in those
   224	  four shapes only. No test pins this, and `canonical_edit_completed` is
   225	  outside `_CRITICAL_SECURITY_ACTIONS`, with no default reader. It is
   226	  not coverage.
   227	- **The forensic detector can have false positives** (the regex is
   228	  intentionally loose). Logging a false positive costs nothing —
   229	  blocking one breaks user velocity. Separating the two lets us
   230	  tune each independently.
   231	
   232	**Composition invariant:** the two detectors share
   233	`_CANONICAL_GUARDS` via delayed import from `check_canonical_edit`.
   234	Extending the guard list (e.g. PLAN-089 Wave A's `_KERNEL_PATHS`
   235	expansion) automatically widens BOTH detectors' surfaces with no
   236	code change required.
   237	
   238	**Decoupling test contract:** the matcher patch (Wave B.3) MUST NOT
   239	modify `check_bash_canonical_forensic.py` and MUST NOT change the
   240	PostToolUse-always-allow invariant. Verified by
   241	`test_bash_canonical_forensic.py` (advisory-allow assertion) +
   242	`test_bash_canonical_interceptor.py::test_parse_failure_fails_closed`
   243	(blocker fail-CLOSED assertion). Both tests must remain GREEN.
   244	
   245	---
   281	NOT covered. The E3 lexer does not model the field separator, and the
