   283	
   284	### §6.6 Indirect execution and computed write target — NOT covered
   285	
   286	The matchers compare literal tokens of the raw text, in one spelling.
   287	Two classes escape that comparison, and neither is covered generically
   288	today. The cure is tracked in `.claude/plans/PLAN-195-bash-guard-indirect-execution.md`.
   289	
   290	**Class A — indirect execution of a destructive command.** The trio
   291	(`_check_rm_rf`, `_check_git_reset_hard`, `_check_git_push_force`,
   292	`check_bash_safety.py:364-465`) reads the leading tokens of each chunk
   293	and never recurses. It has no rule for a verb inside a nested interpreter
   294	body, a dynamic evaluation, a chaining runner or a pipe into an
   295	interpreter. It has none for a command name that an expansion produces.
   296	`_scan_blob` inspects bodies, but seeks only canonical path literals.
   297	
   298	**Direct sibling forms.** The direct forms are blocked only in the
   299	canonical spelling: the verb first in a chunk split on `&&`, `||`, `;` or
   300	`|` (`check_bash_safety.py:275`). The command word and the `git`
   301	subcommand are compared case-sensitively, at fixed positions, after
   302	stripping only four launcher prefixes and leading assignments (`:107`).
   303	Other separators, grouping, reserved words, launchers, command-word case,
   304	options before the subcommand, and disagreements between its tokenizers
   305	do not match.
   306	
   307	**Class B — computed or respelled write target.** E3 compares each
   308	target string with `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` and expands nothing. When a
   309	variable, a glob, a relative directory change or a program argument
   310	produces the destination, E3 never compares the destination itself. A
   311	LITERAL target is compared only in the guard's own spelling: E3
   312	normalizes just a leading `./` and an absolute path under the root
   313	(`check_bash_safety.py:2222-2240`), and the raw segment matcher it calls
   314	is case-sensitive (`check_canonical_edit.py:949-985`). The Edit/Write
   315	classifier folds case; E3 does not use it. Another spelling of the same
   316	path, or a filesystem alias, is not blocked.
   317	
   318	**Detection.** No generic block exists for either class. For Class A
   319	and for a computed target, `PreToolUse` is the only detection, and it
   320	has no rule for them. For a respelled or symlinked literal target, the
   321	forensic hook gives partial detection, after the fact only, and only in
   322	its four literal write shapes (§5); no test pins it and no default
   323	reader consumes the event. Nothing detects copy, move, removal, a hard
   324	link, or the creation of the alias. The OS sandbox is disabled
   325	(`.claude/settings.json`, `sandbox.enabled: false`).
   326	
   327	**Residuals declared by form.** Static shell analysis is incomplete by
   328	construction. These forms stay open even after the planned cure (single
   329	source: the PLAN-195 Goal plus its item C12; items 8–10 await decisions):
   330	
   331	1. Destructive APIs of the interpreter's own language, with no shell verb.
   332	2. A script in a file, including one the Write tool wrote earlier.
   333	3. Functions and aliases from the shell profile or from earlier calls.
   334	4. Tool configuration that runs a configured value as a program.
   335	5. Shell startup-file variables (env-hijack guard, advisory by default).
   336	6. Arguments that reach `xargs` through standard input.
   337	7. A fully computed command word and arguments, with no literal destructive marker.
   338	8. Removal by a file search with a removal action, with or without a predicate.
   339	9. Recursive removal without the force option (allowed by the trio today).
   340	10. Opaque-feeder sub-forms (input the hook cannot read) demoted to advisory, if any.
   341	11. A directory change made in an earlier tool call (the hook gets no working directory).
   342	12. A comment blind spot in the E4 line-break normalizer (posture-toggle guard).
   343	13. A literal target not byte-equal to the guard's spelling: a path-spelling variant, or a filesystem alias made earlier.
   344	
