   401	## What the local override reaches
   402	
   403	It reaches:
   404	
   405	- **Subagents that have no model of their own.** Per the docs (*Create custom
   406	  subagents*, "Choose a model", read 2026-09-22), Claude Code 2.1.280 resolves
   407	  a subagent's model in this order: the model Claude passes for that
   408	  invocation, the definition's `model:` frontmatter (where `inherit` means the
   409	  main session's model), `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, then the main
   410	  session's model. Setting that variable to `inherit` is the same as leaving
    96	   `.claude/settings.json`; anything that can edit that file can remove them.
    97	   In this repo that file is itself canonical-guarded; in target repos the
    98	   installed template ships an `Edit(.claude/settings.json)` deny entry
    99	   (on current CLIs it covers all file-editing tools; PLAN-161 C1 removed
   100	   the unconsulted `Write` twin), which raises the bar but is the same
   101	   class of self-referential guard.
   102	5. **No behavioral certification of enforcement.** Our test
   103	   (`scripts/tests/test-install-deny-baseline.sh`) behaviorally certifies the
   104	   *install mechanism* — entries land, dedup, order, opt-out, exclusions,
   105	   no-jq fallback — by running the real installer. Whether the live harness
   106	   *enforces* a given entry is Claude Code's contract, not this repo's, and
   107	   is not replayable in CI without the harness. We say so instead of
   108	   pretending otherwise.
   109	
   110	## Idempotency and re-runs
   111	
   112	- The injection runs **only when the install run itself created**
   113	  `settings.json`. Re-running `install.sh` hits the existing
   114	  `EXISTS -> SKIP` path, so an entry you deleted is never re-added.
   115	- The merge is order-preserving and deduplicating (template entries stay
   116	  first, baseline entries appended once), so even a forced re-apply cannot
   117	  duplicate entries.
   118	
   119	## How an adopter opts out
   120	
   121	- **At install time:** `CEO_INSTALL_SKIP_DENY_BASELINE=1 ./scripts/install.sh <target> …`
   122	  skips the injection entirely (mirrors `CEO_INSTALL_SKIP_SELF_SHA`). The same
   123	  merge also writes `permissions.defaultMode` = `"manual"` when the
   124	  `settings.json` the run produced has none (a `--ceremony user` install ships
   125	  no `permissions` block), so the opt-out, like the fail-open case below,
   126	  leaves that key unwritten too. With no `defaultMode` in any settings source,
   127	  Claude Code 2.1.295 can open the session in auto mode: its fallback for "no
   128	  source configured a mode" picks auto when, among other conditions,
   129	  `disableAutoMode` is not `"disable"`, the auto-mode killswitch is off, a
   130	  server-side flag (default on) or an account setting allows it, and either
   131	  the session is interactive or a server-side flag that is on by default
   132	  waives that
   133	  (measured in the 2.1.295 binary on 2026-10-09; not a documented contract).
   134	  If you opt out, set `permissions.defaultMode` yourself.
   135	- **After install:** delete any entries you don't want from
   136	  `permissions.deny` in `<target>/.claude/settings.json`. Re-runs of the
   137	  installer will not put them back.
   138	- **Fail-open on infrastructure:** if neither `jq` nor `python3` is available
   139	  at install time, the baseline is skipped with a warning (the settings file
   140	  is left exactly as the template shipped it) — the install never fails
   141	  because of this section.
